ic Irrigation Tutorials Water Holding Home Experiment Irrigation Resources Irrigation Scheduler Mobile Irrigation Schedule Tables Irrigation Fact Sheets Irrigation Glossary Unit Descriptions Unit Conversions Washington Washington Irrigation Water Needs Days-of-Year. 21 Points Days-of-Year. 5 Points Growing-Deg-Dys. 21 Points One Crop, All Stations One Station, All Crops Simple Irrigation Schedules Woodruff Charts for Washington Irrigation System Design Capacity Columbia Basin Soil Survey Available Water Map Irrigation Suggestions By Crop Type WSU Irrigation Research Washington Irrigation Links Idaho Idaho Irrigation Links Oregon Oregon Irrigation Links About Us Contact Us Drip Line Rate The drip line application rate is specific to your irrigation system and shouldn't change, so take a note of it. This constant rate is multiplied by all future irrigation set times to give the net application depths for those sets. Use this form to calculate the water application rate of drip irrigation lines (tape, tubing) given the flow rate from individual emitters , a constant spacing of the emitter s along the drip line, and a constant spacing between the drip lines. Learn more about the units used on this page. Emitter flow rate: gph lph ml per min gpm lps Spacing between emitters in the tubing or tape: in ft mm cm m Distance between drip lines: in ft mm cm m Application Rate: in/hr in/day mm/hr mm/day cm/hr cm/day The Equation This calculator uses this equation to determine the Application Rate of a drip line irrigation system. WSU Prosser - IAREC , 24106 N Bunn Rd, Prosser WA 99350-8694, 509-786-2226, Contact Us",
